B = {
86: dict(e="Cancer cells do two things they shouldn't: divide out of control, and invade other tissues (metastasis). A tumor grows like a bathtub overflowing: either the tap is too high (more division) or the drain is clogged (less apoptosis, the cell's planned self-destruct). Cancer cells also escape the built-in limit on divisions.",
 nw="'Cancer cells can be defined by two fundamental properties: 1. Uncontrolled proliferation; balance between cell division and differentiation (and apoptosis) is lost. Avoid replicative cell senescence. 2. They invade and colonize other tissue - metastasis.'",
 hu="'Avoid replicative cell senescence' = they never run out of divisions (telomeres, card on telomerase)."),
87: dict(e="Benign Behaves: it stays in one lump in its own tissue, above the basal lamina (the thin floor mat under epithelium). Malignant Moves: it breaks through the basal lamina and can spread. Invading is the defining feature of cancer, and the spread tumors (metastases), not the first one, usually kill.",
 nw="'Benign tumor: cells clustered together in a single localized mass in their appropriate tissue... only become problematic if they become so large they interfere with normal functions or secrete excess amounts of biologically active substances.' 'Malignant tumor: cells that have acquired the ability to invade surrounding tissue/other sites in the body (metastasize). Invasiveness an essential characteristic of cancer. Generally metastases that kill cancer patients.' 'Tumor: a clone of cells that proliferate and accumulate in defiance of normal restraints on cell division (neoplasm = new growth).'",
 hu="'Secrete excess amounts of biologically active substances' = makes too much of a hormone or similar. 'In defiance of normal restraints' = ignoring the stop signals."),
88: dict(e="Carcinomas (from epithelium, the cells lining surfaces and organs) are about 80% of human cancers. Why so many? Cancer mutations only matter in cells that divide, and linings like gut, skin and airway divide constantly. Sarcomas come from connective tissue or muscle; leukemias and lymphomas from blood-forming cells.",
 nw="'Carcinomas: cancers arising from epithelial cells; most common cancers in humans (80%). Why? In order for oncogenic mutations to cause cancer they must occur in dividing cells.' 'Sarcomas: cancers arising from connective tissue.' 'Leukemias and Lymphomas: cancers arising from blood-forming cells.'",
 hu=None),
89: dict(e="Name pairs: '-oma' alone is the benign one; add 'carcin' (epithelium) or 'sarc' (connective tissue) and it's malignant. Gland-like epithelium: adenoma (benign) vs adenocarcinoma (malignant). Cartilage: chondroma (benign) vs chondrosarcoma (malignant). One word part flips the answer.",
 nw="'Adenoma = benign epithelial tumor with a glandular organization. The corresponding malignant tumor is an adenocarcinoma. Chondroma = benign tumor of cartilage. Corresponding malignant tumor = chondrosarcoma.'",
 hu=None),
90: dict(e="Normal cells in a dish are polite: they divide a limited number of times, form one flat layer, stop when they touch, need growth factors, and obey stop signals. Cancer cells climb on each other into clumps (foci), divide forever, grow without growth factors, and ignore stop signals. Under a microscope they look rushed: big nucleus, many dividing cells, few specialized features.",
 nw="Normal: 'finite number of divisions, form a monolayer in culture dish, require growth factors in media, are able to respond to growth inhibitory influences.' Cancer: 'pile on top of one another forming clumps, ignore growth inhibitory signals, continue to grow in the absence of growth stimulatory signals.' 'Cancer cells exhibit characteristics of rapidly growing cells: high nucleus to cytoplasm ratio, increased frequency of dividing cells, few specialized structures.'",
 hu=None),
91: dict(e="Cancer cells eat fast food. They gulp glucose and run the quick first stage (glycolysis) hard, but send little through the efficient mitochondrial stage (oxidative phosphorylation). The point isn't energy; it's using the half-processed pieces as building blocks to make more cell. They do this even when oxygen is there. PET scans find tumors with radioactive glucose (fluorodeoxyglucose, FDG).",
 nw="'Cancer cells have altered sugar metabolism - the Warburg effect. Cancer cells have very high sugar uptake and an increase in glycolysis. Use very little for oxidative phosphorylation. Produce small molecule building blocks for macromolecular synthesis.' 'Uptake of radioactively labelled fluorodeoxyglucose.'",
 hu="'Building blocks for macromolecular synthesis' = raw parts for making DNA, protein and fat."),
92: dict(e="A broken enzyme makes a fake key. Mutant isocitrate dehydrogenase (IDH, a citric acid cycle enzyme) makes a new chemical, 2-hydroxyglutarate, which piles up and jams enzymes that need α-ketoglutarate. Many of those enzymes control which genes are on, so a metabolism mutation changes gene expression.",
 nw="'Cancer cells can produce novel metabolites. 70% of brain cancers and ~25% of acute myeloid leukemias have a mutation in isocitrate dehydrogenase. The mutation causes cancer cells to convert isocitrate to a new metabolite, 2-hydroxyglutarate. Accumulates to high levels. Inhibits the activity of many enzymes that require α-ketoglutarate for their function. Many of these enzymes control gene expression.'",
 hu="Her slide says '70% of brain cancers'. The real figure applies to certain brain cancers (lower-grade gliomas), but on her exam, '70%' with 'brain cancers' is what she expects."),
93: dict(e="Telomeres are the plastic tips on a shoelace: TTAGGG repeats at chromosome ends that get shorter every division. Telomerase is the repair kit that adds them back. Embryo, germ and stem cells make it; most body cells barely do, so they retire after a set number of divisions (fibroblasts: 25-50). That counter is replicative senescence. Cancer cells keep telomerase on and never retire.",
 nw="'Telomeres are repetitive DNA sequences at the end of linear chromosomes. During replication, the enzyme telomerase adds TTAGGG repeats.' 'Embryonic cells, germ cells and stem cells produce telomerase, while dividing somatic cells produce very little. Therefore, telomeres shorten with each cell cycle and cells will quit dividing after a specific number of divisions. This cell division counting mechanism is called replicative senescence. Human fibroblasts undergo 25-50 doublings then stop dividing.'",
 hu=None),
94: dict(e="Every tumor comes from one original cell (clonal origin). Proof: in chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML), chromosomes 9 and 22 swap pieces and leave a short 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome), and every leukemia cell has the exact same break point. Same chipped tooth = same family. But one mutation alone still isn't enough for cancer.",
 nw="'Most cancers initially derived from a single abnormal cell.' 'Primary tumor is thought to derive by cell division from a single cell that initially experienced some heritable or environmental change - clonal origin.' 'All leukemic cells will have the same translocation breakpoint, indicating that the cancer originated from a single aberrant cell. However, a single mutation is not sufficient to transform a normal cell into a cancerous cell!'",
 hu="'Aberrant' = abnormal. 'Transform' = turn into a cancer cell."),
95: dict(e="One mutation rarely causes cancer; you need several stacked up in one cell's family over years, like a car crash needing a stuck pedal AND failed brakes AND no mechanic. Evidence: cancer climbs steeply with age, mice with both myc and rasD get tumors much faster than either alone, and colon cancer shows the mutations arriving step by step. A detected tumor has usually been growing for years and has a billion or more cells.",
 nw="'A single mutation is not generally sufficient to cause cancer. After the first mutation, additional changes accumulate in some of the descendants of this cell allowing them to out-grow, out-divide, outlive neighboring cells and become metastatic. By the time a tumor is detected, a typical human cancer will have been developing for many years and will already contain a billion cells or more. Cancer usually originates in proliferating cells.'",
 hu=None),
96: dict(e="Colon cancer goes in order: A Kid Should Panic. APC lost first (a small polyp starts), then K-ras turned on (the adenoma grows), then Smad4 lost on chromosome 18 (the TGFβ brake is gone, a bigger adenoma), then p53 lost last (it becomes a carcinoma). Chromosomes 5, 12, 18, 17. p53 is famous but comes LATE.",
 nw="'Successive oncogenic mutations can be traced in colon cancer.' L9's figure labels the chromosome 18 step 'loss of tumor suppressor in DCC region'; L11's updated figure renames it 'loss of Smad4 and other tumor suppressors'.",
 hu="Same step, two names depending on which lecture's figure you're shown: 'DCC region' (L9) and 'Smad4' (L11). Both mean the chromosome 18 loss."),
97: dict(e="Cancer cells have a broken spell-checker. They pick up mutations faster than normal cells, usually because genes that repair DNA (caretakers) are themselves broken. More typos means more chances to hit the next gene the tumor needs. Their chromosomes look chaotic (single-letter changes, extra copies, missing chunks, wrong chromosome numbers), and different cells in one tumor end up different (heterogeneity).",
 nw="'Human cancer cells are genetically unstable - this instability can speed up tumor progression: accumulate genetic changes more rapidly than normal cells, often unable to repair DNA damage or replication errors. These defects can be due to mutations in DNA maintenance genes.' 'Tumors contain point mutations, DNA amplifications and deletions, and aberrant number of chromosomes (whole or in part). Rapid changes in the genome can lead to heterogeneity of cancer cells within a tumor.'",
 hu=None),
98: dict(e="A normal epithelial cell knocked out of place falls up into the hollow tube (the lumen) and dies, because it loses the 'you're attached, stay alive' signals from the matrix. A cancer cell falls DOWN into the tissue (basal extrusion) and survives, because it turns up its own survival signals and dodges apoptosis.",
 nw="'Some mutations allow cancer cells to cross the basement membrane.' Figure: normal cells extrude apically into the lumen and die; cancer cells extrude basally and survive.",
 hu="'Basement membrane' and 'basal lamina' are the same floor mat; she uses both names."),
99: dict(e="Spreading takes six steps: Let go (lose cell-cell adhesion), Break out (through the basal lamina), Hop on (enter a blood vessel), Ride (fewer than 1 in 1000 cells survive this), Hop off (stick to a vessel wall far away and squeeze out), Settle in (grow into a new tumor). Very few make it, but metastasis causes about 90% of cancer deaths.",
 nw="'Metastasis - spread of cells through the blood stream from their sites of origin to other sites in the body. Very few cells actually survive to colonize another tissue. Metastasis is responsible for 90% of cancer associated deaths.'",
 hu=None),
100: dict(e="A tumor is a city that needs roads. Without its own blood vessels it stalls at about 2 mm, because the middle runs out of food and oxygen. So tumors release growth factors (bFGF, TGFα, VEGF) that make nearby vessels sprout toward them (angiogenesis). Proof: tumors in a perfused thyroid with no vessels stopped at 1-2 mm; tumors in the eye's fluid stayed tiny but grew fast on the iris, which has vessels.",
 nw="'Tumors induce angiogenesis; the formation of new blood vessels. Without blood vessels to supply nutrients, tumors only grow to about 2 mm in diameter. Many tumors secrete growth factors such as bFGF, transforming growth factor α (TGFα) and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). These factors induce angiogenesis.' 'Two experiments illustrating the requirement for angiogenesis.'",
 hu=None),
101: dict(e="HIF is the 'Help, I'm Fainting!' alarm. When oxygen is low, HIF is active and switches on VEGF to call for blood vessels. When oxygen is high, HIF gets an -OH tag (hydroxylation), the VHL part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase spots the tag, and HIF is shredded. So oxygen keeps the alarm off.",
 nw="'VEGF expression can be induced by hypoxia.' 'HIF - hypoxia inducible factor; a transcription factor activated in low oxygen conditions. HIF stimulates the transcription of VEGF and other genes whose products are required when oxygen is limiting. When O2 levels are high, HIF is modified by hydroxylation and then targeted for degradation by the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway.'",
 hu="'Hypoxia' = low oxygen. 'Modified by hydroxylation' = gets an -OH tag, which is the 'destroy me' signal here."),
102: dict(e="VHL is the garbage collector for HIF. In von Hippel-Lindau syndrome, VHL is broken, so HIF is never destroyed, even with plenty of oxygen. The cell thinks it's always short on oxygen, keeps making VEGF, and grows hemangioblastomas: tumors packed with blood vessels. HIF isn't made more; it just isn't thrown out.",
 nw="'Mutations in a specific E3 ubiquitin ligase subunit is defective in von Hippel-Landau (VHL) syndrome. Produce large quantities of HIF regardless of O2 levels. The result is the production of hemangioblastomas, tumors that contain dense mass of blood vessels.'",
 hu="'Produce large quantities of HIF' sounds like more is being made. It's really that HIF isn't being destroyed. (Her slide also spells it 'Landau'; the name is Lindau.)"),
}
